<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anwaruddin Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1672–<a href="/years/1749">1749</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Muhammad Anwaruddin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anwaruddin Khan was Nawab of the Carnatic, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcot">Arcot</a>, from 1744 to 1749, installed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asaf-jah-i">Asaf Jah I</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-of-hyderabad">Nizam of Hyderabad</a>. In 1746 his large army was routed by a small French force at the Adyar river, an early sign of the superiority of European-trained troops. He was killed at the Battle of Ambur in 1749 fighting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanda-sahib">Chanda Sahib</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muzaffar-jung">Muzaffar Jung</a>, who were backed by the French.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anwaruddin-khan.jpg" alt="Anwaruddin Khan" width="320" height="461" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paul Philippoteaux · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Death_of_the_Nabob_of_the_Carnatic_by_Paul_Philippoteaux.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Anwaruddin Khan was born in 1672 at Gopamau, in the Hardoi district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a>, into a family of Qannauji Sheikhs; his father was Haji Muhammad Anwar ud-din Khan. He went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, enlisted in the imperial army and rose quickly. He received the title Anwar ud-din Khan Bahadur from Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>, Shahamat Jang from Emperor Shah Alam I and Siraj ud-Daula from Emperor Muhammad Shah, and became the right-hand man of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asaf-jah-i">Asaf Jah I</a>, the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-of-hyderabad">Nizam of Hyderabad</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subedar">Subedar</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thatta">Thatta</a> from 1721 to 1733, faujdar of Srikakulam, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajahmundry">Rajamahendravaram</a> and Machlipatnam in 1724, governor of Eloore and Rajamundry after 1725 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazim">Nazim</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a> from 1725 to 1743, and at times Naib-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wazir">wazir</a> of the empire. On 28 March 1744 he was made regent of the Carnatic during the minority of Saadatullah Khan II, and in July 1744 the Nizam appointed him Nawab of the Carnatic, making him the founder of its second dynasty.</p><p>When the First Carnatic War broke out between the French and the English in 1746, he had warned both against fighting on his land. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-francois-dupleix">Joseph François Dupleix</a>, the French governor-general, placated him with the offer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a>, but withdrew it once the French had taken the town. Anwaruddin then sent 10,000 men under his son Mahfuz Khan to take it, and they were beaten by 300 French soldiers on the Adyar River.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Courted by both companies, he sided with the English, and the French backed Husayn Dost Khan, called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanda-sahib">Chanda Sahib</a>, as the rightful Nawab. After the death of the Nizam in 1748 the succession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a> was disputed between his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasir-jung">Nasir Jung</a> and his grandson Muzaffar Jang, who came south and allied with Chanda Sahib and the French. Anwaruddin met their army at Ambur on 3 August 1749 and was killed in the battle at the age of 77. Ripley's Believe It or Not later listed him as the oldest soldier to die in battle, wrongly giving his age as 107.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anwaruddin Khan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Gopamau, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Ambur, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=27.55,80.28&amp;year=1722" aria-label="Gopamau on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6658 1154 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Anwaruddin Khan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7108 1424h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7108 1424h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=27.55,80.28&amp;year=1722">What was here in 1722? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
